1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,350
There is a way of telling a story that
makes almost anything look like a mystery.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:06,430
It has three steps.

3
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:09,860
Once you have seen them,
you cannot unsee them.

4
00:00:10,490 --> 00:00:14,280
They are in documentaries, in
headlines, in entire TV channels.

5
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:20,170
The man who perfected the method
sold seventy million books.

6
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:24,110
Before we get to those three steps,
there is something you should know.

7
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:29,300
In November 1968, Swiss police
arrested Erich von Däniken.

8
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:31,420
Not for anything he had written.

9
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:33,630
For forging hotel paperwork.

10
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:38,400
He had faked invoices and credit
references for twelve years

11
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:42,307
to take out loans worth a hundred
and thirty thousand dollars.

12
00:00:42,757 --> 00:00:44,367
He spent the money on travel.

13
00:00:45,137 --> 00:00:48,547
On the very journeys described in the
book that had come out that March.

14
00:00:49,767 --> 00:00:55,117
In 1970 a court gave him three and a half
years for repeated and sustained fraud.

15
00:00:55,967 --> 00:00:57,857
He wrote his next book in prison.

16
00:00:59,507 --> 00:01:03,837
So. Does that prove his theories about
ancient aliens were a deliberate fraud?

17
00:01:05,067 --> 00:01:07,077
No. It proves he committed fraud.

18
00:01:07,747 --> 00:01:09,117
Those are two different claims.

19
00:01:10,114 --> 00:01:11,514
And the gap between them...

20
00:01:11,594 --> 00:01:14,184
the jump you almost made a second ago...

21
00:01:14,354 --> 00:01:16,764
is what this whole video is about.

22
00:01:18,274 --> 00:01:22,044
Because that jump is the move
he made for a living. Not once.

23
00:01:23,174 --> 00:01:25,444
Thousands of times, in front
of millions of readers.

24
00:01:28,151 --> 00:01:31,361
So let's take it apart.
How do you sell a mystery?

25
00:01:33,721 --> 00:01:35,851
Step one. Start with something real.

26
00:01:36,781 --> 00:01:38,521
Not a hoax. Not a forgery.

27
00:01:38,601 --> 00:01:41,911
Something a reader can visit,
photograph and measure.

28
00:01:42,821 --> 00:01:44,371
The Nazca Lines, for instance.

29
00:01:45,521 --> 00:01:47,251
They are real. They are enormous.

30
00:01:48,121 --> 00:01:52,111
Hundreds of lines and figures scraped
into a desert plateau in southern Peru.

31
00:01:53,311 --> 00:01:56,721
Step one is free. You have
not claimed anything yet.

32
00:01:57,131 --> 00:01:59,401
You have simply pointed
at a thing that exists.

33
00:02:00,661 --> 00:02:03,631
Step two. Point at something
we do not fully understand.

34
00:02:04,741 --> 00:02:06,771
And here you still do not have to lie.

35
00:02:07,561 --> 00:02:09,901
Why people made lines
best appreciated from

36
00:02:09,981 --> 00:02:12,711
the air is a real archaeological question.

37
00:02:13,911 --> 00:02:16,601
Two steps in, every
single word has been true.

38
00:02:17,291 --> 00:02:19,201
That is what makes the method work.

39
00:02:19,991 --> 00:02:21,741
The trouble, if any, comes next.

40
00:02:22,601 --> 00:02:26,411
Step three. Ask whether the explanation
might be something extraordinary.

41
00:02:27,731 --> 00:02:29,401
Perhaps they were landing strips.

42
00:02:31,781 --> 00:02:35,331
Notice what happened there.
Nobody claimed anything.

43
00:02:36,001 --> 00:02:36,961
A question was asked.

44
00:02:37,041 --> 00:02:38,891
And questions cannot be wrong.

45
00:02:39,891 --> 00:02:42,721
Meanwhile the lines do
not need visitors at all.

46
00:02:43,471 --> 00:02:46,301
Archaeologists found the
wooden stakes at their ends.

47
00:02:47,381 --> 00:02:49,201
They found offerings left on them.

48
00:02:49,281 --> 00:02:51,141
Pottery. Textiles.

49
00:02:51,221 --> 00:02:55,111
And that most of the figures are
drawn with one unbroken line.

50
00:02:55,911 --> 00:02:57,631
The technique is not exotic either.

51
00:02:57,711 --> 00:03:00,571
Stakes, cord, and the patience to clear

52
00:03:00,651 --> 00:03:03,121
dark stones off the
pale ground underneath.

53
00:03:04,411 --> 00:03:07,521
But nothing in the archaeology
suggests a landing strip.

54
00:03:08,651 --> 00:03:10,281
And notice what that took.

55
00:03:11,051 --> 00:03:14,181
We did not have to explain
every line out there to say it.

56
00:03:14,971 --> 00:03:18,721
Rejecting one explanation is not
the same as having all the answers.

57
00:03:20,501 --> 00:03:24,071
But that answer takes a minute,
and the question took four words.

58
00:03:24,381 --> 00:03:27,151
Hold on to that, because
it comes back later.

59
00:03:30,121 --> 00:03:33,741
And the shape repeats everywhere.
The pyramids. Mayan monuments.

60
00:03:33,821 --> 00:03:37,571
Old religious texts. Real
thing. Open question. Aliens?

61
00:03:38,494 --> 00:03:43,114
Take Palenque. The lid of a Maya
ruler's sarcophagus shows a man who

62
00:03:43,194 --> 00:03:46,044
looks remarkably like somebody
sitting inside a machine.

63
00:03:47,684 --> 00:03:51,574
Däniken saw an astronaut. A control
panel. An engine. A spacecraft.

64
00:03:52,564 --> 00:03:55,824
Archaeologists see the tomb of
Pakal the Great, who ruled that

65
00:03:55,904 --> 00:03:59,414
city for sixty-eight years and
died in the seventh century.

66
00:04:00,744 --> 00:04:05,054
They see a world tree. The open
jaws of a funerary serpent.

67
00:04:05,604 --> 00:04:09,154
A king wearing the attributes
of the Maya maize god.

68
00:04:09,604 --> 00:04:12,674
And their objection is not that
it looks nothing like a rocket.

69
00:04:13,584 --> 00:04:16,934
It is that the rocket reading comes
from looking, and nothing else.

70
00:04:18,004 --> 00:04:19,914
Not the burial. Not the inscriptions.

71
00:04:20,884 --> 00:04:24,534
Not everything else those same symbols
meant on every other Maya carving.

72
00:04:25,724 --> 00:04:28,474
The object is real. The
strangeness is real.

73
00:04:29,124 --> 00:04:30,734
The extra step is the reading.

74
00:04:33,614 --> 00:04:35,404
So was Däniken simply lying?

75
00:04:37,634 --> 00:04:40,384
The evidence does not give
us a comfortable answer.

76
00:04:41,444 --> 00:04:43,344
A con man takes the money and stops.

77
00:04:43,424 --> 00:04:46,714
Däniken kept going for six decades.

78
00:04:47,444 --> 00:04:50,274
He kept writing and arguing
long after he was rich.

79
00:04:51,334 --> 00:04:56,554
In 2018, aged eighty-three, he said
contact would come within ten years.

80
00:04:56,634 --> 00:05:01,541
And in the same interview:
"For this I have no proof."

81
00:05:03,241 --> 00:05:07,131
He died in January 2026, aged ninety.

82
00:05:08,051 --> 00:05:11,281
That does not look like a
simple one-time publishing scam.

83
00:05:12,111 --> 00:05:15,601
And yet the conviction is real,
and it does tell us one thing.

84
00:05:16,251 --> 00:05:19,321
He was willing to falsify
documents when it suited him.

85
00:05:20,261 --> 00:05:24,091
Both of those can be true at once,
and that is the uncomfortable part.

86
00:05:25,901 --> 00:05:27,911
Because the method does not need a liar.

87
00:05:28,681 --> 00:05:31,271
It works perfectly well in
the hands of a believer.

88
00:05:32,471 --> 00:05:34,601
A believer picks the best examples.

89
00:05:35,471 --> 00:05:37,691
A believer leaves out
the inconvenient context.

90
00:05:37,771 --> 00:05:41,661
And can mislead people without
ever thinking of it as lying.

91
00:05:42,801 --> 00:05:45,691
And that is the distinction
the whole thing turns on.

92
00:05:46,441 --> 00:05:49,851
Believing something sincerely,
and handling evidence honestly,

93
00:05:50,771 --> 00:05:52,741
are two completely different skills.

94
00:05:53,511 --> 00:05:57,061
You can have the first and fail the
second without ever telling a lie.

95
00:05:59,971 --> 00:06:01,551
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

96
00:06:01,631 --> 00:06:04,381
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

97
00:06:05,231 --> 00:06:06,701
It really helps the channel.

98
00:06:08,091 --> 00:06:11,081
And then television arrived
and discovered something worse.

99
00:06:12,201 --> 00:06:14,691
The method does not
need a believer either.

100
00:06:17,041 --> 00:06:19,971
Ancient Aliens has been
running since 2010.

101
00:06:20,621 --> 00:06:23,211
The same three steps. Every week.

102
00:06:24,071 --> 00:06:25,521
Find something impressive.

103
00:06:25,771 --> 00:06:27,261
Note something unexplained.

104
00:06:27,931 --> 00:06:30,561
Move past the ordinary explanation.

105
00:06:31,071 --> 00:06:32,661
Ask whether it could be aliens.

106
00:06:33,643 --> 00:06:36,083
Add ominous music. Cut to the sky.

107
00:06:36,163 --> 00:06:39,593
Go to the advert break. Then do it again.

108
00:06:40,403 --> 00:06:42,573
And here is the part
that makes it a business.

109
00:06:43,543 --> 00:06:45,453
The show does not have to prove aliens.

110
00:06:46,323 --> 00:06:48,313
It only has to make the answer dull.

111
00:06:49,243 --> 00:06:51,773
Picture an episode about
an ancient monument.

112
00:06:52,543 --> 00:06:54,873
The exciting version takes twenty seconds.

113
00:06:56,043 --> 00:07:00,913
Nobody knows exactly how every stone
was moved. Therefore... aliens?

114
00:07:01,944 --> 00:07:04,364
The ordinary version needs
generations of accumulated

115
00:07:04,444 --> 00:07:08,954
skill, thousands of workers,
ramps, ropes and organisation.

116
00:07:10,494 --> 00:07:12,184
It needs the archaeology.

117
00:07:12,574 --> 00:07:15,794
The workers' village beside
the pyramids. Their bakeries.

118
00:07:15,874 --> 00:07:17,284
Their healed broken bones.

119
00:07:18,293 --> 00:07:20,753
It needs the experiments in
which people actually moved

120
00:07:20,833 --> 00:07:24,383
blocks weighing several tonnes
using wood, water and rope.

121
00:07:25,643 --> 00:07:27,053
It is a better answer.

122
00:07:27,883 --> 00:07:30,893
It is also much harder to turn
into an hour of television.

123
00:07:33,733 --> 00:07:36,603
Which brings back the thing
I asked you to hold on to.

124
00:07:37,392 --> 00:07:40,902
There is a documentary called
Ancient Aliens Debunked.

125
00:07:41,252 --> 00:07:43,362
It takes the claims one at a time.

126
00:07:44,382 --> 00:07:46,732
It runs for three hours
and eleven minutes.

127
00:07:47,872 --> 00:07:49,862
Twenty seconds to raise the question.

128
00:07:50,632 --> 00:07:52,402
Three hours to answer it properly.

129
00:07:53,292 --> 00:07:54,802
No wonder the question wins.

130
00:07:57,342 --> 00:07:58,912
One note about that film.

131
00:07:59,822 --> 00:08:03,352
Its maker, Chris White, comes from
a Christian apologetics background.

132
00:08:04,782 --> 00:08:08,152
Worth knowing. And it does not
make the archaeology in it wrong.

133
00:08:09,172 --> 00:08:13,022
Any more than a fraud conviction
makes his argument about Nazca wrong.

134
00:08:13,972 --> 00:08:17,342
That argument fails on the
archaeology, not on his record.

135
00:08:18,612 --> 00:08:21,222
Judge the evidence, not
the person holding it.

136
00:08:21,892 --> 00:08:24,762
A rule that only runs
one way was never a rule.

137
00:08:27,112 --> 00:08:29,482
I will put a link to
the documentary below.

138
00:08:31,592 --> 00:08:34,012
Which brings us back to
Däniken, and to something

139
00:08:34,092 --> 00:08:36,942
genuinely odd about
what became of his idea.

140
00:08:38,232 --> 00:08:40,502
He was a traveller. A storyteller.

141
00:08:41,132 --> 00:08:43,262
He was honestly fascinated
by ancient civilisations.

142
00:08:45,221 --> 00:08:47,521
His explanations were poorly supported.

143
00:08:47,601 --> 00:08:49,811
Often demonstrably wrong.

144
00:08:50,481 --> 00:08:53,591
His methods were criticised
for nearly sixty years.

145
00:08:54,581 --> 00:08:56,801
In one book he printed a
photograph of what he called

146
00:08:56,881 --> 00:09:00,031
markings like the parking
areas of a modern airport.

147
00:09:01,301 --> 00:09:04,911
It was a cropped picture of the knee
joint of one of the Nazca birds.

148
00:09:08,161 --> 00:09:12,781
But he did at least take the reader
somewhere. Egypt. Peru. Mexico.

149
00:09:12,861 --> 00:09:14,061
You could feel the dust.

150
00:09:14,851 --> 00:09:18,441
Ancient Aliens took that idea
and built a machine out of it.

151
00:09:19,091 --> 00:09:20,641
Find something strange.

152
00:09:21,371 --> 00:09:23,021
Leave out what does not fit.

153
00:09:23,891 --> 00:09:27,681
Ask whether aliens could
explain it. Add the music.

154
00:09:28,071 --> 00:09:29,661
And do it again next week.

155
00:09:30,801 --> 00:09:35,971
So in a strange way, the show does not
preserve his legacy. It cheapens it.

156
00:09:36,731 --> 00:09:39,001
The man had one extraordinary idea.

157
00:09:40,791 --> 00:09:44,041
Television discovered that the
idea did not need an answer.

158
00:09:44,791 --> 00:09:46,521
It only needed another episode.

159
00:09:49,781 --> 00:09:51,801
So the next time something
asks you whether it could

160
00:09:51,881 --> 00:09:54,891
be, you already know which
step you are standing on.

161
00:09:55,901 --> 00:09:58,451
And there is nothing wrong
with saying we do not know.

162
00:09:59,041 --> 00:10:01,611
It is often the most
honest sentence available.

163
00:10:02,741 --> 00:10:05,151
It is not a hole that
something has to fill.

164
00:10:05,881 --> 00:10:08,771
It is the reason the ancient
world is worth studying.

165
00:10:10,631 --> 00:10:13,341
We do not need aliens.
We already have people.

166
00:10:14,211 --> 00:10:15,881
And people are strange enough.

167
00:10:17,391 --> 00:10:19,921
Because the mystery was
never really the aliens.

168
00:10:21,081 --> 00:10:23,091
The mystery is why we kept watching.
